title: Interdisciplinary Studies SBA, Part 2 - Developing a Problem Statement
authors: EDUC 3100 Assessment Module
~~~~

Orientation [id=orientation2]
-----------

Omer: Welcome to Part 2 Module of the Interdisciplinary Research Process (IRP) Scenario-based Activity.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> interdisciplinary_orientation2

Omer: Part 2 should take around 45 minutes to an hour, and you can break it up over several days if you'd like. It is due November 23.

Omer: Remember that I will be seeing these results, so please take it seriously and do your best work. I think you'll enjoy it.

Introduction to Part 2 [id=interdisciplinary_introduction2]
------------------------

Omer: You've done good work in Part 1. Let's recap where we are.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> interdisciplinary_recap

Omer: Let's see if you remember the disciplines for each insight. **For each insight, select each discipline that applies.**

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> insight_review

::insight_review [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 label="Select Relevant Disciplines for Each Insight"]

--- wait @insight_review.submitCount > 0 ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> initial_dropout_cause_diagram

Omer: If you remember from Repko et al. (2025), we can use mapping to integrate
insights from different disciplines which will help us achieve a more comprehensive
understanding of the problem. Please add the links you think we should add to the diagram.

--- wait @initial_links_done.value ---

Ty: I'm done.

Peggy: So am I!

Lacy: Me too.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> dropout_cause_mapping

Omer: Thank you for your contributions. The map to the right correctly summarizes
the causal connections we have already discussed.

Lacey: Yes, I remember we talked about mapping in class but I don't remember
what kind of map this is supposed to be. It is either a **multidisciplinary**
map or an **interdisciplinary** map.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> classify_map

Lianne: What do you think? And what is the difference between a multidisciplinary
and an interdisciplinary map anyway?

--- wait @cause_mapping_choice.value && @cause_mapping_choice_explanation.value ---

Ty: I checked my notes and found out that this is what Dr. Ari said is
a **multidisciplinary map** where each interdisciplinary insight is used
to explain only a part of the problem.

Peggie: Exactly! Because of that, it does not show us how these insights relate
to one another. That is what we need to do to gain a comprehensive understanding
of the problem.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> dropout_cause_mapping

Lianne: Great! Let's see how we can integrate these insights.

Lianne: Why don't you brainstorm and we'll compare notes? Try to decide which
of the facts are likely to interact or affect one another in important ways,
and if so, take notes about how that might happen.

Lacey: OK, let's see ... Students working long hours most likely come from families low on family assets.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_1

Lianne: That is right. We can show that relationship by drawing an arrow from
**Family and Peer Factors** to **Students Working Long Hours** like this (look to the right).

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_2_mermaid

Ty: That makes sense. I am sure students working long hours are also trying
to support their families if they come from families with low assets. So the
arrow should go the other direction as well. We can show it this way (look to the right).

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_mapping_2

Lianne: Yes, exactly! Integration is helping bring these disparate insights together for
a broad-view understanding of the causes of this problem. (See diagram to the right.)

Lianne: How about we start a summary of these connections? We will use
this summary as the end of Part 3 where we will write a problem statement.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_mapping_introduction

Peggie: Great! I started an introduction.

Lacey: Thank you, Peggy. This introduction looks great. Now let's
add a sentence about the connections we just made.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_2

Peggy: How about the points you and Ty made earlier with a sentence like the one shown to the right?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_mapping_with_first_crosslink

Lianne: That sounds great. I added that sentence to our summary.

Lacey: Thanks, Lianne.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> dropout_factor_combos

Lianne: Your turn. Why don't you start by deciding which connections between
factors you need to add. (Use the form to the right.)

--- wait @dropout_factor_checkboxes.value && (@dropout_factor_combo_1_explanation.value || @dropout_factor_combo_2_explanation.value || @dropout_factor_combo_3_explanation.value || @dropout_factor_combo_4_explanation.value || @dropout_factor_combo_5_explanation.value) ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> revise_dropout_cause_diagram

Lianne: Good! Now you need to revise the diagram so that it shows the
connections you've chosen. It may take you a few minutes to get the
hang of it, but you can directly edit the code for the diagram in
the screen to the right.

--- wait @extra_links_done.value ---

Ty: I'm done.

Peggy: So am I!

Lacy: Me too.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> extra_cause_connections

Lianne: Looking over your suggestions, I see three more connections we should make.
They make the most sense considering the studies we reviewed.

We've already worked on connections between **Family Peer Factors** and
**Students Working Long Hours**. How about we work on explaining the
remaining connections?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> factors_1_to_4
 	
Lianne: Let's start with this one.

How about we each write a sentence to explain the connection we see.
Write your explanation in the box to the right.

--- wait @cause_connection_3_explanation.value ---

Ty: I've finished my explanation.

Lacy: So have I.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_3_explanation_choice

Lianne: Good work. Here are all the explanations for this connection. Now we need to select the one we need to include in our summary. Which one
would you select to include in our summary?

--- wait @cause_connection_3_explanation_choice_input.value && @cause_connection_3_choice_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> factors_2_to_3

Lianne: Let's do the same thing for this one too. Write a sentence to explain
the connection you see in the box under it.

--- wait @cause_connection_4_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_4_explanation_choice

Lianne: Good work. Here are all the explanations for this connection.

Now we need to select the one we need to include in our summary. Which one
would you select to include in your summary?

--- wait @cause_connection_4_explanation_choice_input.value && @cause_connection_4_choice_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> factors_3_to_4

Lianne: Let's do the same thing for the third connection too. Write a sentence to explain
the connection you see in the box under it.

--- wait @cause_connection_5_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_connection_5_explanation_choice

Lianne: Good work. Here are all the explanations for this connection. Now we need to select the one we need to include in our summary. Which one would you select to include in your summary?

Lianne: I appreciate the thoughtfulness you've all shown on this exercise,
both as individuals and as a team.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> overview_of_connections

Lianne: I reviewed the explanations you wrote. I think the sentences shown to the right
do a good job of explaining the three connections we worked on. These sentences
cite detailed information from studies that shed light on the nature of these
connections. I will add these sentences to our summary.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> extended_summary

Lianne: Integrating disciplinary insights like this helps us see the big picture -- often revealing what's missing.

What insight(s) do you think  are missing that we should include here to broaden the understanding that we have from this exercise?

Ty: Looking at the map, I see that **Family and peer factors** and **Equitable school funding** are insights with the most connection to other insights.

Lacey: That is right. They have the most outgoing connections. Also, they appear to reflect structural causes of high school dropout.

Peggy: We should look for additional insights from disciplines that may study structural issue.

Ty: But how do we do that?

Peggy: Maybe we should find and analyze some other articles that specifically look at how these or other factors interact.

Lacey: Yes, of course. Let's do that.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> reading_funding_text

Ty: Using keyword search I found this study. Let's see which disciplines are represented in this study.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> reading_funding_checkboxes

Lianne: So which discipline or disciplines do you think are represented in the study?

--- wait @reading_funding_classification.value && (@rf_bio_explanation.value || @rf_econ_explanation.value || @rf_health_explanation.value || rf_polisci_explanation.value || @rf_psych_explanation.value || @rf_soc_explanation.value) ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> reading_funding_highlighted

Lianne: Let's think about the insight of this source just like we did with the other studies.

Remember that disciplinary "insight" is often a recommendation, main finding, or conclusion
offered by the authors of the disciplinary source and it reflects the perspective of the discipline.

I have highlighted the words that I think show what discipline is most relevant to this study -- Economics.

Ty: This is a great study on effects of school funding and their effects on economic and educational outcomes.

Lacy: Yeah, great job finding it, Ty! It just says 10% increase in per-pupil spending increased schooling raising likelihood of high school graduation.

Peggy: And the longitudinal data shows participants who were exposed to that school funding increase saw increased wages and family income when they reached adulthood. Poverty rates were lower too because of it.

Ty: Most importantly, low income students saw the largest gains.

Lacy: Yes! It says in the results section that these gains extend to structural mechanisms like smaller class sizes, longer school years, and higher education salaries.

Peggy: That is important. That makes me think schools will have more capacity to address individual and special needs
of students like those with ADHD or those who fall behind because they have to work long hours.

Lianne: Great discussion, everyone! We'll need to capture all of those points in a sentence that will serve as the
insight of this source.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> reading_funding_insight

Lianne: Why don't we each write our own sentence just like we've been doing in other studies?

--- wait @reading_funding_insight_sentence.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> reading_funding_insight_choice

Lianne: Good work. Here are the sentences you all suggested. Which one should we go with?

--- wait @reading_funding_insight_choice_input.value && @reading_funding_insight_choice_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> best_reading_funding_insight_sentence_display

Lianne: Good work, everyone! The sentence on the right captures all the important points from the study
and we should use it as the insight for this source.

Ty: Great! We should add this insight to our map.

Peggy: We could label this insight on our map "School Finance Reforms".

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> revise_dropout_cause_diagram_with_finance_reform

Lianne: Why don't you try adding this insight to the map. You'll have to both add a node with the label, and add connections.

--- wait @extra_links_done_finance_reform.value ---

Lacey: I considered putting a connection directly from "School finance reform" to "Dropping out of high school", but that didn't make sense. The obvious thing that school finance reform is change the status of "Equitable school funding". So I added a link from "School Finance Reform" to "Equitable School Funding".

Ty: I thought about those things too, but I also noticed in the passage the following quote: "**Mechanisms: Spending increases translated into smaller class sizes, longer school years, and higher teacher salaries, which contributed to improved student outcomes.**" All of those are things that are made possible by equitable school funding.

Peggy: There's another possible connection, though. If schools have more money, they can do things like more outreach to families, hire more school counselors, do more after-school programs, and things like that. Which would create a connection from "School Finance Reform" to "Family and Peer Factors".

Ty: But that isn't in the article! If we're going to add that connection, we have to be able to provide evidence from studies, not just pull it out of our heads. And anyway, those are direct results of equitable funding, **not** a direct result of school finance reform. School finance reform produces better funding, and **that** is what reduces dropout rates.

Lacey: Well, it does say that school finance reform leads, over the long term, to higher adult wages, greater family incomes, and lower poverty rates. That certainly is an effect on "Family and Peer Factors"!

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> cause_mapping_4

Peggy: I think that works. So that gives us the diagram shown to the right. It's interesting that school finance reform doesn't affect graduation rates directly. It's an **indirect** influence, through its effects on schools and families.

Ty: That means we need to add explanations for the two connections we've added to our summary.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> overview_of_connections_2

Lianne: That's right! But you've already pretty much said what they are, so I'll write them up real fast. (Take a look to the right).

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar2 -> extended_summary_2

Lianne: So here's how the summary looks now. (Take a look to the right).

Ty: Adding this new insight on **School finance reforms** has definitely enriched my understanding of causes of this problem.

Lacey: I agree. I broadened our understanding to the larger structural issues around policy reform.

Lianne: Good work! That ends Part 2 of the SBA. Let's recap what we've done so far.

We've built an interdisciplinary map of causes of high school dropout using insights from the studies we have reviewed. That allowed us to achieve a more comprehensive understanding of the problem and revealed that an important insight was missing. **School finance reforms**. We were able to add that insight to our map and our updated summary.

In Part 3, we will explore other aspects of the problem and define the scope of what our nonprofit can do to address it in our community. We will synthesize all this in a problem statement to be submitted to our director.

Nice job, everyone!

**However, there's one more thing to do.**

Go to the next page, where you will find a report for you to share with your teacher. Click on the **Print or save as PDF** button and save that report as a PDF file on your computer. Then upload it to your part 2 assignment in iCollege.
